  <section id="average" aria-labelledby="average-h">
    <h2 id="average-h">Average Waist Size for Women in the U.S.</h2>
    <p>The average American woman aged 20 or older has a <strong>38.5&nbsp;inch (98&nbsp;cm)</strong> waist, according to the most recent National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES, August 2021&ndash;August 2023). The median is about 37.5&nbsp;inches, so half of U.S. women measure more and half less. The same survey puts the average woman at 5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;3.5&nbsp;in (63.5&nbsp;in) and 171.8&nbsp;lb.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/average-waist-size-women-chart-3d.webp" width="1760" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Waist size chart for women: 3D figures with 28 to 44 inch waists and their percentiles"><figcaption>A 5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;3.5&nbsp;in woman with waists from 28 to 44 inches, and how many U.S. women measure less.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Where women&rsquo;s waist sizes rank</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Waist (tape at navel)</th><th scope="col">Percentile, U.S. women 20+</th><th scope="col">Health risk band</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">28 in (71 cm)</th><td>3rd</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30 in (76 cm)</th><td>7th</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">32 in (81 cm)</th><td>15th</td><td>Increased</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">34 in (86 cm)</th><td>27th</td><td>Increased</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">35 in (89 cm)</th><td>33rd</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">36 in (91 cm)</th><td>40th</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">38 in (97 cm)</th><td>53rd</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">40 in (102 cm)</th><td>63rd</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">42 in (107 cm)</th><td>73rd</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">44 in (112 cm)</th><td>81st</td><td>High</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>These numbers often surprise people, because the waist sizes quoted in fashion (24, 26 or 28&nbsp;inches) are <em>natural</em> waists, measured at the narrowest point. Health surveys measure lower, at the top of the hip bone, which gives a bigger number. Compare like with like: see <a href="#natural-waist">natural waist vs. health waist</a> below.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="by-age" aria-labelledby="by-age-h">
    <h2 id="by-age-h">Average Waist Size for Women by Age</h2>
    <p>Women&rsquo;s waists grow most between the 20s and the 50s. The table shows the median (middle) waist and the range covering the middle half of women in each age group.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Women&rsquo;s waist size by age (NHANES 2021&ndash;2023)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Median waist</th><th scope="col">Middle half</th><th scope="col">5th / 95th percentile</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">20-29</th><td>33.6 in (85.3 cm)</td><td>30.4&ndash;38.8 in</td><td>27.0 / 50.1 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">30-39</th><td>36.7 in (93.1 cm)</td><td>33.0&ndash;42.8 in</td><td>28.6 / 52.6 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">40-49</th><td>37.7 in (95.7 cm)</td><td>33.5&ndash;43.0 in</td><td>29.6 / 51.5 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">50-59</th><td>39.7 in (100.8 cm)</td><td>34.8&ndash;44.3 in</td><td>30.6 / 51.5 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">60-69</th><td>39.2 in (99.6 cm)</td><td>35.0&ndash;43.5 in</td><td>30.4 / 49.4 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">70-79</th><td>38.9 in (98.9 cm)</td><td>35.1&ndash;42.7 in</td><td>30.2 / 48.7 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">80+</th><td>37.2 in (94.4 cm)</td><td>35.0&ndash;42.2 in</td><td>30.1 / 46.9 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>The median rises by about 3&nbsp;inches from the 20s to the 30s, a period that includes pregnancy for many women, and by another 2&nbsp;inches around the 50s, when menopause shifts fat storage toward the abdomen. After 60 the median levels off and then falls slightly.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="by-height" aria-labelledby="by-height-h">
    <h2 id="by-height-h">Ideal Waist Size for Women by Height</h2>
    <p>A taller woman naturally has a bigger frame, so the most useful target scales with height. Research by Ashwell and others supports a simple rule: <strong>keep your waist to less than half your height</strong>. A waist-to-height ratio of 0.5 or more is linked with higher cardiometabolic risk across ages and ethnic groups.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Waist size targets for women by height</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Height</th><th scope="col">Keep waist under (0.5)</th><th scope="col">Lean to healthy (0.45&ndash;0.5)</th><th scope="col">High risk (0.6+)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">4 ft 10 in</th><td>29.0 in</td><td>26.1&ndash;29.0 in</td><td>34.8 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 0 in</th><td>30.0 in</td><td>27.0&ndash;30.0 in</td><td>36.0 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 2 in</th><td>31.0 in</td><td>27.9&ndash;31.0 in</td><td>37.2 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 4 in</th><td>32.0 in</td><td>28.8&ndash;32.0 in</td><td>38.4 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 6 in</th><td>33.0 in</td><td>29.7&ndash;33.0 in</td><td>39.6 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 8 in</th><td>34.0 in</td><td>30.6&ndash;34.0 in</td><td>40.8 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 10 in</th><td>35.0 in</td><td>31.5&ndash;35.0 in</td><td>42.0 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6 ft 0 in</th><td>36.0 in</td><td>32.4&ndash;36.0 in</td><td>43.2 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Try your own numbers in the <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-height ratio calculator</a>, which also explains the 0.4 and 0.6 lines.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="health" aria-labelledby="health-h">
    <h2 id="health-h">What Waist Size Is Healthy for a Woman?</h2>
    <p>Waist size is one of the simplest ways to spot belly fat, the visceral fat around the organs that is linked with insulin resistance, high blood pressure and heart disease. The WHO and the U.S. National Institutes of Health use these cut-offs for women:</p>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>Under 31.5&nbsp;in (80&nbsp;cm):</strong> low risk from waist size.</li>
      <li><strong>31.5&ndash;35&nbsp;in (80&ndash;88&nbsp;cm):</strong> increased risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Over 35&nbsp;in (88&nbsp;cm):</strong> substantially increased risk.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Because the average U.S. woman now measures 38.5&nbsp;inches, more than half of women are above the high-risk line. Waist size matters even at a normal BMI: a woman with a normal weight but a large waist has a higher risk than her BMI suggests. Shorter women should also check the waist-to-height ratio, since 35&nbsp;inches is a lot more waist on a 5&nbsp;ft frame than on a 5&nbsp;ft&nbsp;9&nbsp;in one. To see how much of your weight is fat, use the <a href="/body-fat-percentage-chart/">body fat percentage chart</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="natural-waist" aria-labelledby="natural-waist-h">
    <h2 id="natural-waist-h">Natural Waist vs. Health Waist</h2>
    <p>Women usually have two quite different &ldquo;waists&rdquo;:</p>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>Natural waist:</strong> the narrowest part of the torso, a little above the navel. Dress and pants size charts, sewing patterns and body-shape tools such as our <a href="/">body type calculator</a> use this one.</li>
      <li><strong>Health waist:</strong> at the top of the hip bone, around navel level. NHANES, the NIH and most doctors use this one, and so do the averages and percentiles on this page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The gap between the two is bigger in women with a defined, hourglass-type waist and smaller in women who carry weight around the middle. If you only know your natural waist, your health waist is larger, so measure again at the navel before using the calculator. For the full set of tape positions, see our <a href="/body-measurements/">body measurements guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-measure" aria-labelledby="how-to-measure-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-measure-h">How to Measure a Woman&rsquo;s Waist</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/how-to-measure-waist-women-3d.webp" width="1400" height="880" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Where to measure a woman&amp;rsquo;s waist: natural waist, top of the hip bone and hips, on a 3D woman"><figcaption>The natural waist, the top of the hip bone (NHANES) and the hips on the same woman.</figcaption></figure>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Find the top of the hip bone.</strong> Stand up straight and feel for the top of your hip bone (iliac crest) on the side of your body. For most women it is close to navel level, below the narrowest part of the waist.</li><li><strong>Wrap the tape.</strong> Wrap a soft tape measure around your body at that level, keeping it parallel to the floor all the way around. Use a mirror to check the back.</li><li><strong>Breathe out normally.</strong> Relax, breathe out normally and read the tape at the end of the breath. Do not suck in your stomach or pull the tape tight.</li><li><strong>Record and repeat.</strong> Take two readings and use the average. Measure at the same time of day each time, ideally in the morning, and not during pregnancy.</li></ol>
    <p>Stick to one method when tracking progress. Your waist and hip measurements together also give your <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-hip ratio</a> and your <a href="/">body shape</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="men" aria-labelledby="men-h">
    <h2 id="men-h">How Women Compare With Men</h2>
    <p>The average U.S. man has a 40.6&nbsp;inch (103&nbsp;cm) waist, about 2&nbsp;inches more than the average woman, even though he is about 5&nbsp;inches taller. Health cut-offs for men are higher: 37&nbsp;in (94&nbsp;cm) for increased risk and 40&nbsp;in (102&nbsp;cm) for high risk. See the full men&rsquo;s data in <a href="/average-waist-size-men/">average waist size for men</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lose" aria-labelledby="lose-h">
    <h2 id="lose-h">How to Reduce Your Waist Size</h2>
    <p>Spot-reducing does not work: crunches strengthen the muscles under the fat but do not burn the fat on top. What shrinks the waist is losing body fat overall, which takes a modest calorie deficit, enough protein to keep muscle, resistance training two to four times a week, regular walking and enough sleep.</p>
    <p>Use the <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a> to set calories and protein, and the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a> to see what a smaller waist would look like on your frame.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Women&rsquo;s Waist Size FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average waist size for women?</h3></summary><p>The average waist size for women in the United States is 38.5 inches (98 cm), measured at the top of the hip bone, according to NHANES August 2021–August 2023 data for women aged 20 and over. The median is about 37.5 inches.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is a 28 inch waist small for a woman?</h3></summary><p>It depends on where it was measured. A 28 inch natural waist, the narrowest point used for dress sizes, is slim but common in young, lean women. Measured at the navel the way health surveys do it, 28 inches is very small: only about 3% of U.S. women measure less.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is a 32 inch waist good for a woman?</h3></summary><p>Measured at the navel, a 32 inch waist is smaller than about 85% of U.S. women. It sits just above the 31.5 inch (80 cm) line where health risk starts to rise slightly, and well under the 35 inch (88 cm) high-risk line. For a woman 5 ft 4 in or taller it also keeps the waist-to-height ratio at or under 0.5.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What waist size is unhealthy for a woman?</h3></summary><p>The WHO and the U.S. National Institutes of Health use 35 inches (88 cm) as the point where the risk of type 2 diabetes and heart disease is substantially increased for women, with risk starting to rise from about 31.5 inches (80 cm). The International Diabetes Federation uses 31.5 inches (80 cm) for women of all ethnic groups.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the ideal waist size for a woman by height?</h3></summary><p>A simple rule is to keep your waist under half your height (a waist-to-height ratio under 0.5). That means under 31 inches at 5 ft 2 in, under 32 inches at 5 ft 4 in, under 33 inches at 5 ft 6 in and under 34 inches at 5 ft 8 in.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is my dress size waist the same as my health waist?</h3></summary><p>No. Clothing size charts use the natural waist, the narrowest part of the torso, which can be several inches smaller than the waist at the navel that doctors and health surveys use. Use the navel measurement for health checks and the natural waist for clothes.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Does waist size increase after menopause?</h3></summary><p>Often, yes. Around menopause, falling estrogen shifts fat storage from the hips and thighs toward the abdomen, so many women see their waist grow even when their weight changes little. The median U.S. woman’s waist rises from about 33.6 inches in her 20s to about 39.7 inches in her 50s.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is a normal waist-to-hip ratio for a woman?</h3></summary><p>The WHO uses a waist-to-hip ratio of 0.85 or more as a sign of abdominal obesity in women. Many healthy women fall between 0.70 and 0.80. You can check yours with the waist-to-hip ratio calculator.</p></details>
  </section>

